## Why the cell reframe is technically superior, not just thematically nicer

The rheology gave us how the medium moves. It said nothing about how a region decides what it is — how a dropped CSV becomes a chart. That was a genuine gap in the model, and I'd have had to invent machinery to fill it. The cell metaphor fills it with an existing, rigorous concept: a cell has a genotype (the data/schema it absorbed) and a phenotype (the UI it manifests), and the map between them is expression. That's not decoration. It gives us a second formal axis the fluid model didn't have:

* Rheology axis — geometry over time. Where things are, how they move, settle, lock. Herschel-Bulkley, solidity, diffusion. (Already mapped.)
* Genetic axis — identity and manifestation. What a region is, what controls it grows, how two regions share behavior. Phenotype expression, trait inheritance, symbiosis. (New, and it's the part that makes "digests any data asset" a mechanism instead of a wish.)

> One is how it flows, the other is what it becomes. A UI cell is fully specified only by both. That's why the reframe is structural: it doubles the model's expressive power along an axis that was previously empty.

## The genetic axis, formalized

* Every cell carries a genome — the schema/traits it has absorbed — and expresses a phenotype — its current rendered controls and skin. The expression map is a lookup, not magic:
phenotype = express(genome)

* Genetic Asset Assimilation = on drop, the cell sequences the asset (schema detection: MIME type, structure, column types) and writes the result into its genome. This is a pure, testable function: bytes in, trait-set out. No physics, no ambiguity.
* Phenotypic Refactoring / Polymorphic Rendering = express() maps a trait-set to a component phenotype (tabular genome → chart phenotype; video genome → player phenotype). Crucially this is a registry of expressions, extensible without - touching the core — new data type = new entry, exactly like adding a gene to a lookup table. That extensibility is the whole "plug-and-play" claim made concrete.
* Symbiotic Binding = when two cells share an interface (the rheological boundary from before), they can also exchange genes across it — trait flow, not just pressure balance. The fluid boundary and the genetic boundary are the same boundary doing two jobs. That coincidence is the tell that the two axes are correctly unified rather than bolted together.

